{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~3–10 EUR), especially in downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the entire path when recent history shows falling momentum.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: after a regime change from high-70s/low-80s to low-70s/high-60s, the model decays too slowly. Increase the speed of mean reversion downward when the last 5–10 days show consecutive declines or a break below recent support.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: the model tends to project recoveries back toward mid/high-70s (and even low-80s) that do not materialize. Cap rebound magnitude when the market has recently gapped down; enforce weaker bounce elasticity (e.g., only partial retracement).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: bias increases with forecast horizon (later days drift higher than outcomes). Apply a horizon-based downward adjustment that grows with lead time (small at 1–5d, larger at 20–30d).",
    "Volatility miscalibration in selloffs: the model underestimates the depth of drawdowns (realized tails lower). When recent realized volatility rises and direction is negative, widen downside moves and skew the distribution lower rather than centering around the prior mean."
  ]
}
